#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
jthread-cpu - correlate JVM threads (jstack) with per-thread CPU usage.

The JVM prints a native thread id in every jstack entry as `nid=0x<hex>`
(JDK 21+ prints it in decimal - both are handled).  On Linux that value is the
kernel TID, which is exactly what `top -H -b -p <pid>` reports in its PID
column.  This script samples one, parses the other, joins them on that id and
prints something a human can read at 3am.

On macOS there is no `top -H` and nid is a mach port rather than a TID, so the
portable `jstack` method is used instead: two thread dumps a few seconds apart,
differencing the per-thread cumulative `cpu=` field.  Same output, no top.

Sampling methods
----------------
  top     `top -H -b -n2 -p PID`  -> interval %CPU per TID          (Linux)
  proc    /proc/PID/task/*/stat   -> interval %CPU per TID          (Linux)
  jstack  two Thread.print dumps  -> delta of the `cpu=` field      (portable,
                                     works on macOS, target JDK 11+)

CPU units
---------
  Per-thread %CPU is measured like `top`: 100% = one core fully busy, so a
  single thread tops out near 100%.  The banner also reports the total in
  cores and as a share of the CPU capacity actually available to the JVM
  (host CPUs, narrowed by CPU affinity and any cgroup CPU quota).

Examples
--------
  jthread-cpu                           # autodetect the only JVM, 2s sample
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 -n 10            # show top 10 hottest threads
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 -d 5 -f 12       # 5s sample, 12 stack frames each
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 -c 6 -i 10       # 6 rounds, 10s apart, then a summary
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 --watch          # redraw until Ctrl-C
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 -o json          # machine readable (see --print-schema)
  jthread-cpu --jstack-file d.txt --top-file t.txt   # offline post-mortem
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 --theme latte    # catppuccin latte, for light terminals
  jthread-cpu -p 12345 --ascii          # no box-drawing glyphs

Requires only the standard library.  Python 3.7+.
"""

